    "title": "18 States to Ban SNAP Recipients from Using Benefits for Some Junk Food in 2026",
    "text": "• Starting January 1, 2026, some states will ban SNAP recipients from using benefits to buy certain foods.\n• Indiana, Iowa, Nebraska, Utah, and West Virginia are the first of at least 18 states to enact waivers.\n• Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. and Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins have urged states to restrict unhealthy foods from the SNAP program.\n• The aim is to reduce chronic diseases like obesity and diabetes.\n• Retail industry and health policy experts express concerns about preparedness for the changes.\n• The National Retail Federation predicts longer checkout lines and customer complaints.\n• SNAP is used by 42 million Americans.\n• Nearly 62% of SNAP participants are in families with children.\n• Roughly 14% of U.S. households reported food insecurity between January and October.\n• Implementing SNAP restrictions could cost U.S. retailers $1.6 billion initially and $759 million annually.\n• The Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 allows SNAP benefits for 'any food or food product intended for human consumption,' except alcohol and ready-to-eat hot foods.\n• Under the second Trump administration, states have been encouraged to seek waivers.\n• The five state waivers taking effect January 1 affect about 1.4 million people.\n• Utah and West Virginia will ban soda and soft drinks, Nebraska will prohibit soda and energy drinks, and Indiana will target soft drinks and candy.\n• Iowa's rules affect taxable foods, including soda and candy, and certain prepared foods.\n• SNAP waivers enacted now and in the coming months will run for two years, with the option to extend them for an additional three.\n• Each state is required to assess the impact of the changes.",
